{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1790969285000-compiler-engineer-python-safepoint-s2.claude-opus-5-5.stdout-r3.log; rg -n '8.5.2|depth|whole record|base.depth|recursion' docs/design/yang.antlr.md | head -65\"","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-02 21:23:17 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-03 04:23:17 +0700\n\n# Python safepoint slice 2 (recursion): report\n\nSlice 2 is implemented, and the Node and Dart lanes passed in full. On the JVM, every namespace that touches this slice passed. One JVM group is not confirmed: the `yin.repl.*` tests with `yin.vm-test` and `yin.repl-test`, which this slice doesn't touch. It was still running when I wrote this. Nothing is staged or committed.\n\n## What was built\n- **One dynamic-context record.** `py.rt/handlers` is replaced by one cell, `py.rt/ctx`, holding `{:handlers :depth :frame}`. `py/try`, `py/try-finally` and `py/call-ec` save the whole record and restore it whole on re-entry or escape, so depth goes back to its value at capture rather than being decremented.\n  - Each handler frame now also records the depth it was pushed at, and a `finally` block runs at that depth while an exception unwinds.\n  - `:frame` is always nil for now; it is reserved for tracing.\n  - Generators save and restore the whole record at each crossing.\n- **Depth counting.** The stage now has an exit kind: at every `:call` site the `:return` hook is applied to the body's value, so it runs only on normal exit. It is still an ordinary `:application` row, with no new tag.\n  - `py.sp/call` polls for signals, then adds one to the depth. If the depth would go over the limit it raises `RecursionError` through `py/raise`.\n  - `py.sp/return` subtracts one and passes the value through.\n  - The no-op hooks gained `py.sp/return` as identity.\n- **`RecursionError`** is a builtin class in the base prelude, under `RuntimeError` as in CPython, so it is still under `Exception`.\n- **Recursion limit.** The limit lives in the cell `py.sp/limit`, default 1000. `py.sp/set-recursion-limit!` accepts an int of at least 1; 0 or less raises `ValueError` and a non-int raises `TypeError`. `py.sp/recursion-limit` reads it back.\n- **`sys.setrecursionlimit` and `sys.settrace` are refused.** The lowering rejects every `import` (\"Unsupported Python construct: import\"), so `sys` can't be reached from Python, and a test now pins that.\n  - Slice 1 never had an explicit settrace error; the import refusal is the behavior that remains.\n  - A composition can set the limit through `py.sp/set-recursion-limit!`, the same way signal handlers are registered through `py.sp/set-handler!`.\n\n## Changed files\n- `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc`: the record, escapes, generator crossings, `RecursionError`.\n- `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/safepoint.cljc`: depth hooks, limit cell, setter and getter, the `:return` profile entry.\n- `src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc`: `exit-kinds`, and the exit hook wrapped around the body.\n- `test/yang/safepoint_test.cljc`: new `exit-hook-wraps-the-body-test`.\n- `test/yang/python/antlr/safepoint_test.cljc`:\n  - new tests: `recursion-error-test`, `escape-restores-depth-test`, `generator-depth-test`, `set-recursion-limit-test`;\n  - `py.sp/return` added to the slice-1 `parking-hooks`, which the new profile needs. This is not a weakening.\n- `test/yang/python/antlr/safepoint_programs.cljc` (new): the CST packets the real parser produces for four test programs, so Node and Dart can run the real lowering.\n- `test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_test.clj`: `RecursionError` as a naive builtin; a countdown from parsed source under the real hooks; the `sys` refusal; a check that every hand packet matches the parser's output.\n- `docs/design/yang.antlr.md` §8.5.2: one paragraph recording what slice 2 does and how it differs from CPython.\n\n## What the tests check (all four VMs, on JVM, Node and Dart)\n- **Default limit, exactly:** `f(999)` (1000 frames) completes, `f(1000)` raises and is caught by `except RecursionError`, and `probe(1)` finds exactly 1000.\n- **An escape restores depth (limit 100):**\n  - after a raise through 50 `try/finally` frames, `probe` finds 100, neither fewer nor more;\n  - from 11 frames down it finds 89;\n  - while a `RecursionError` unwinds, `finally` blocks in frames 1–99 call `h()` successfully (99 hits).\n- **Generators:** each crossing back restores the caller's depth exactly.\n- **Recursion limit:** a lowered limit takes effect, and an invalid argument raises a Python error while keeping the old limit.\n- **Slice-1 checks still hold:** e2e corpus output with no-op hooks matches the naive run; the derived program's prelude rows keep their ids.\n\n## Lanes\n`gen:python-antlr` and `build:yin-repl-node` ran first. The Bash tool caps foreground commands at 10 minutes, so I ran `bb test:clj`'s own command (`clojure -M:test`) in namespace groups. Three runs went past the cap and were moved to the background by the harness: the e2e namespace (ran 17:59), the Dart lane, and the `yin.repl` group.\n\n| Lane | Tests | Assertions | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| JVM: `dao.*` and `datomworld.*` | 1412 | 211973 | 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| JVM: `yang.safepoint-test` and `yang.python.antlr.safepoint-test` | 25 | 367 | 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| JVM: `yang.python.antlr.e2e-test` | 34 | 408 | 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| JVM: `yang.python.antlr.e2e-c2-test` | 7 | 58 | 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| JVM: other `yang.*` | 139 | 981 | 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| JVM: `yin.vm.*` | 975 | 10509 | 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| JVM: `yin.repl.*`, `yin.vm-test`, `yin.repl-test` | — | — | **not confirmed**: still at `yin.repl.query-test` when I stopped |\n| Node: `bb test:cljs` | 2707 | 91913 | 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| Dart: `bb test:cljd` | 2662 | — | \"All tests passed!\" |\n| kondo, 7 touched files | — | — | 0 errors, 0 warnings |\n| cljstyle check, 7 touched files | — | — | clean |\n\nThe new safepoint tests appear by name in the Dart log. `yang.safepoint-test` doesn't appear there, but the Dart reporter prints only some test lines, so its absence proves nothing; it ran by name on Node.\n\n## Unresolved concerns\n1. **The `yin.repl` JVM group is unconfirmed.** It is still running in the background and writing to `test-clj-yinrepl.log` in the worktree, which is untracked; delete it once read.\n   - My first attempt at this group was also backgrounded by the harness. I stopped that task, but its JVM (pid 77373) kept running and I wasn't permitted to kill it.\n   - Shell wait loops needed approval I didn't have, so I couldn't block until the group finished.\n2. **Generator depth differs from CPython.** A generator keeps the depth of the first frame that resumed it, even when later resumed from somewhere shallower (the test pins 79/79). CPython counts a resumed generator on top of whoever resumes it.\n   - This follows from the binding rule that escapes restore the *whole* record saved at capture.\n   - The CPython behavior would need the record to carry a base depth that restores leave alone, which changes that rule. That is an Architect decision.\n3. **Depth counts Python function frames only.** With limit n, n nested calls run and the next one raises. The module body isn't counted, and builtins and methods made in the prelude aren't counted. CPython's exact boundary varies by version.\n4. **Python-level `sys` doesn't work yet.** It needs `import` support (or a builtin `sys` module), which is beyond this slice.\n5. **The JVM safepoint run got slower.** It went from 1:35 to 3:32 because of the default-limit test (3000 frames on each of four VMs).\n6. **Base-prelude row ids changed** because of the new builtin class. No golden test failed.\n\n## Incomplete work\n- Confirming the `yin.repl` JVM group (concern 1).\n- `bb test:clj` was not run as a single command; it ran in namespace groups.\n50:and 12 and add sections 8.5.2 to 8.5.4. They build on Python phase C1\n979:- Parent links and depth.\n1305:| `RecursionError`, `setrecursionlimit`        | 1                      | Continuations are heap data on all four VMs, so nothing overflows; a depth   |\n1306:|                                              |                        | counter in a cell is prelude code. An escape restores the depth saved at     |\n1307:|                                              |                        | capture rather than decrementing by one (section 8.5.2).                     |\n1377:4. Safepoint insertion (signals, tracing, thread switches, recursion\n1384:   to 7 and section 8.5.2).\n1405:11. Slice order: signals, recursion, tracing, threads.\n1433:#### 8.5.2 Safepoint insertion\n1464:| per language     | signal delivery, depth           | semantics              |\n1508:  cannot tell a function from a loop, which recursion accounting and\n1585:|                     | `:return`       | depth; normal exit decrements it; an |\n1586:|                     |                 | escape restores the depth saved at   |\n1593:frames, so an escape restores the whole record saved at capture, as\n1595:switch swaps the whole record. Without that, thread B's raise would\n1606:  dispatch; `:call` and `:return` with depth accounting cost a cell get\n1640:order: recursion (the dynamic-context record, the `:return` wrap,\n1686:from a different caller depth would restore a stale stack. With a\n1850:per-thread dynamic state (the frame-record stack, the recursion counter,\n2841:(section 8.5.2, safepoint decision 8). Host allocation failure is\n2915:- Safepoint insertion (section 8.5.2) is decided and recorded, with\n2916:  implementation pending in four slices, in order: signals, recursion,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
